
"smart_sources:2005-06-04 - John Mullan created road with future travelers in mind.md": {"path":"2005-06-04 - John Mullan created road with future travelers in mind.md","last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"last_read":{"hash":"16dq9u8","at":1785795005913},"class_name":"SmartSource","last_import":{"mtime":1783634164115,"size":10624,"at":1785795006202,"hash":"16dq9u8"},"blocks":{"#---frontmatter---":[1,9],"#":[10,76],"##{1}":[16,76]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Jeri Mccroskeyjeri Mccroskey","target":"Jeri Mccroskeyjeri Mccroskey","line":5},{"title":"\nA stone marker and sign sit at the Fourth of July pass east of Coeur d'Alene, one of the historic sites associated with Capt. John Mullan who oversaw the building of a road from Missoula to Walla Walla in 1862. \n (Jesse Tinsley / The Spokesman-Review)","target":"https://thumb.spokesman.com/50LKyTnkgNGIZ0AQOISIZkiDz5I=/400x0/media.spokesman.com/photos/2005/06/04/hx_mullan_06-04-2005_SQ4LTO7.jpg","line":10,"embedded":true},{"title":"X","target":"https://twitter.com/share?text=John%20Mullan%20created%20road%20with%20future%20travelers%20in%20mind&url=https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2005/jun/04/john-mullan-created-road-with-future-travelers-in/","line":16},{"title":"\nA stone marker and sign sit at the Fourth of July pass east of Coeur d'Alene, one of the historic sites associated with Capt. John Mullan who oversaw the building of a road from Missoula to Walla Walla in 1862. \n (Jesse Tinsley / The Spokesman-Review)","target":"https://thumb.spokesman.com/50LKyTnkgNGIZ0AQOISIZkiDz5I=/400x0/media.spokesman.com/photos/2005/06/04/hx_mullan_06-04-2005_SQ4LTO7.jpg","line":20,"embedded":true}],"metadata":{"title":"John Mullan created road with future travelers in mind","source":"https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2005/jun/04/john-mullan-created-road-with-future-travelers-in/","author":["[[Jeri Mccroskeyjeri Mccroskey]]"],"published":"2005-06-04","created":"2026-07-09","description":"East of Coeur d'Alene, Interstate 90 parallels the historic John Mullan military wagon road as it winds more than 3,000 feet upward through the Fourth of July Pass to its summit. The pass and summit were so named because on July 4, 1861, Lt. John Mullan and the 200 soldiers and civilians under his command engaged in constructing the road, and camped and spent the day near the site where a monument now stands. In honor of the holiday, Mullan, according to his own official report, gave \"the working parties extra issues of molasses, ham, whiskey, flour and pickles … and spent the day pleasantly and harmoniously in camp…\""},"task_lines":[16],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},"smart_blocks:2005-06-04 - John Mullan created road with future travelers in mind.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"1wup2k8","at":1785795005913},"key":"2005-06-04 - John Mullan created road with future travelers in mind.md#---frontmatter---","lines":[1,9],"size":910,"outlinks":[{"title":"Jeri Mccroskeyjeri Mccroskey","target":"Jeri Mccroskeyjeri Mccroskey","line":5}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
